wincan
=========

A small candle chart window for Windows, with its server.

  candled        (the server) runs on the machine with the raw candle files; it reads them (read-only), aggregates them
                 with rolling windows and serves the candles as JSON over HTTP.
  wincan.exe  (the window) 300x175 pixels, no frame, border or title bar, always on top, never takes the keyboard until it is
                 clicked, and gives it back when the mouse leaves.  Candles and last price of one coin; Up/Down changes the
                 timeframe (1m 3m 5m 15m 30m 1h 4h 1d 1w), Left/Right the coin; type an entry price (e) and a size (s) and it
                 shows the profit or loss in green or red, as a percentage and in dollars; e opens an editor for the position (price, amount, leverage, realized P\&L; it resumes at the box last edited) and x closes the trade at the mark and appends it to a log with the best and worst the trade went through.  Tray icon and Ctrl+Alt+C hide and
                 show it.

See wincan(1), candled(8) and wincan(5) for the details, and INSTALL to build and run them.

The files that candled reads are the ones written by a tick collector: 30-byte frames, '{' + 28 bytes + '}', a candle for every
100 ms and a timestamp marker once a minute (candled(8) says exactly).  The window draws the candles itself from the JSON, so
nothing but the numbers crosses the network.

Limits
------
Without timestamp markers (the start of an old file, a collector that has stopped) the file's modification time stands for the end of
the data; bars older than an interruption of the collector have their order and shape right but their clock times drift by the
length of the gap.  The server has no authentication: allow only the machines that should ask.

Tests
-----
make check   aggregation against a brute-force computation on synthetic files, including a partial frame in the middle of a file.
The window itself needs a person: the look at 300x175, the drag, the keyboard and the focus rules (wincan(1)).
